+# Native Throwdown integration audit and implementation
+
+The supplied Game Modes bundle is revision `f3316c1`. Its referee and wire format implement ten modes. The current workspace already implements a separate native 1-Up coordinator, a native three-card Throwdowns page, private setup/HUD models, exact-build native flag placement, native start transitions, and stock solo cleanup. These working systems remain authoritative.
+
+The imported implementation uses trainer telemetry and its own `0xD5`/version 9 relay messages. It has no supported public DLL registration API. ReSkate's runtime DLL and overlay DLL communicate through existing callbacks; their exports and the session protocol must remain unchanged. Registration will therefore be internal, with validated challenge definitions and adapters rather than speculative external module loading.
+
+## Integration plan
+
+1. Preserve the stock S.K.A.T.E., Spot Battle, Skate Jam, and current 1-Up entry paths and UI ownership.
+2. Reuse the supplied rules/runtime for Spot Jam, Hall of Meat, Deathrace, Domination, Graffiti, Skate Tag, Infection, and Hide & Seek. Register the existing native S.K.A.T.E. and 1-Up as canonical implementations; do not add competing cards for their imported alternatives.
+3. Generate additional native cards from a validated registry. Stable IDs, deterministic palettes, native artwork/fallbacks, and distinct adapters drive card creation and selection. Do not load images or generate art through an external service at runtime.
+4. Present configuration, lifecycle state, scores, timers, standings and cancellation through existing native widget models. Do not compile or activate the supplied Game Modes overlay menu/HUD renderers.
+5. Route only mode messages through the imported decoder. Preserve the current stock and 1-Up relay handling, exact solo pending-queue retirement, and native state cleanup.
+6. Coordinate active-mode/input/marker ownership so incompatible challenges cannot run concurrently. Map changes and missing dependencies must cancel cleanly and report why.
+7. Run rules/registry/lifecycle tests, build the supported DLL, then validate the real game flow. Report observed results separately from untested multiplayer behavior.
+
+## Requirements retained from the earlier project
+
+1-Up uses 1–6 players, 20-second turns by default, no rounds slider, no scoring circle, landed skating trick points without the multiplier, three 1/U/P penalties and last-player-standing elimination. Native game UI components and authored start/countdown animations remain the presentation foundation. The exact solo Throwdowns unlock fix must remain intact. The native in-match Throwdown Panel/Quit route is unfinished in the starting snapshot and remains required.
+
+## Import record
+
+`../throwdown-framework-import.json` records every originally imported file and its hash. The original supplied source/history remains untouched in `../game-modes-import/`. Imported code can be adapted to current runtime contracts, but existing hooks and engine headers are not overwritten wholesale.
+
+## Validation status
+
+The Release runtime and launcher built successfully on 2026-10-09. Eleven checks passed: mode rules/wire, challenge registry, Hall of Meat, 1-Up rules/wire, native Throwdown lifetime, 1-Up coordinator, flag placement, native menu browser/tools/lifetime, and Throwdown relay. The coordinator and placement checks include mutual exclusion with a running new mode. Existing CMake incremental-build warnings about directory dependencies remain; there were no compiler or linker errors in the final build.
+
+The current installed DLL, launcher, bundle hashes and verified backup path are recorded in `../native-throwdown-registry-deploy.json`. The reproducible installation script is `../deploy-native-throwdown-registry.ps1`. Earlier candidate DLL `9876900674cce3aa62cd342f3a6c8c2ce8be110895ebf0aad907d7f6842cb5f4` was rejected after a native crash when opening the picker; it is not a verified release.
+
+## Logo picker correction, 2026-10-09
+
+The challenge picker mounts the game's `GridList_Widget` with its native `GridListViewModel`: static segment count four, vertical orientation, twelve tile handles. Each tile uses a private `LogoCaptionTile_Widget` derived from the native `ContentPresenterCombination_Widget`, a private full-size `LogoTexture_Widget` derived from the native Texture widget, and the native SmallNotice widget for its bottom name strip. Stock S.K.A.T.E., Spot Battle and Skate Jam use private tile copies with their original navigation actions and images. 1-Up and the eight registered modes retain their own callbacks. The original three-item list and category models are left unchanged. Cleanup detaches the private grid before releasing its models, and Back restores the grid selection.
+
+Grid cells are 424 by 424 native UI units, with 32-unit gaps; the total is 1792 by 1336. The grid uses the original mode selector's vertical anchor and a centered horizontal anchor. It is shorter than the original 1374-unit card row, preserving the native header/footer space. A logo occupies (52,16,320,320); the name strip occupies (0,344,424,72). These fixed native reference-element bounds leave 16 units above the logo, 52 at each side, eight between logo and caption and eight below the caption. The caption uses H52-XBold_White, centered, with SmallNotice_RectStretch_Dark_NoIcon as its override. SmallNotice's base Style may read Default after the widget binds; StyleOverride is the relevant field. The approved nine private icons are embedded in the core asset bundle; no new image iteration or third-party HUD was added.
+
+The first caption attempt nested an ordinary LinearList_Widget inside each square. The user's screenshot confirmed that the nested native measurement put the caption over the logo and clipped its top. A subsequent direct-layer attempt also failed visually: the labels stayed at the top and covered almost all the artwork. The current private blueprint puts both references inside one `RimeContainerElementData`, with the layer pointing to that container. Fixed sizes and normalized center anchors describe each region; offsets are zero and sizing pivots are one-half. The stock combination blueprint and gameplay HUD are not modified.
+
+The 2026-10-10 crash dump reported an access violation at `Skate.exe+0x2b68151` while native property dispatch dereferenced a null metadata result. The malformed candidate also put `InterfaceDescriptorData` in the entity Components list and removed the native focus, raw-context and lifetime graph. The corrected private blueprint keeps eight entity components, nineteen property connections, five focus/context links and two lifetime events. Its interface remains owned by Blueprint.Interface. Donor boxed values are decoded before cloning, and the author validates the serialized component types. Crash evidence is preserved in `../throwdown-fixed-tile-crash-analysis.txt`, `../throwdown-fixed-tile-crash-stack.txt`, and `../re-tools/ghidra-throwdown-tile-crash-functions.json`; the exact faulty property lookup was not identified conclusively.
+
+New icons previously failed because `publish_texture` searched the renderer registry before an image had been drawn and registered there. Picker and mode artwork now reads the exact image retained by its private exported category resource, validates its native type, and publishes that image directly into the native texture model. The registry remains a fallback for other images. This prevents a missing first-draw registration from silently retaining the stock donor logo.
+
+The first shared-container build moved the captions to the bottom, as confirmed by the user's screenshot, but its logos flashed briefly and disappeared. The donor has a verified event path `Lifetime -> Delay(0.1s) -> Bool.SetTrue`. Bool.Value then drives both child references' FitHeightToContent. That delayed height override conflicts with the fixed Texture region. The final private tile removes only those two property edges; it retains seventeen other property bindings, the five focus/context links and two lifetime events. Its serialized graph is checked for any remaining FitHeightToContent override. The caption was also raised eight native units at the user's request. Retained live texture models had the correct images, 320-by-320 sizes and native axis defaults, recorded in `../picker-texture-layout-live.log`.
+
+The asset author verifies all ten original dynamic-model defaults are unchanged, adds one private grid default, validates seventeen native binding connections and the lifetime/focus graph, and verifies BC3 alpha for all nine badge textures. It reads the serialized tile back and checks the root-layer-container-child hierarchy, fixed dimensions and normalized anchors for both regions. The compiled patch has 57 resources, ten bundles and eight TOCs. The current embedded archive is 13 files / 92,781,866 bytes with SHA-256 `eaf9bbfc984b0a7f3604858ae3a72da964a3671cd4795a95f20e872d75dcd84f`. Build logs are `../throwdown-tile-visibility-author-build.log`, `../throwdown-tile-visibility-author.log`, `../throwdown-tile-visibility-compile.log` and `../throwdown-tile-visibility-runtime-build.log`. The DLL's exact embedded resource was hash-verified without executing DLL code in `../throwdown-tile-visibility-embedded-check.log`, using `../verify_oneup_embedded_bundle.py`. Eleven unchanged regression checks passed in `../throwdown-tile-crash-fix-tests.log`. The latest visibility correction still requires the user's visual check; no mouse or keyboard automation was performed.
+
+The first grid build fell back to the original three cards for two independent reasons, confirmed from the launch log and read-only process inspection. First, the bundle extractor wrote `Patch/...`, but `one_up_core_assets` mounted the parent cache directory, which retained an older root-level patch. It now mounts the exact `Patch` directory and requires its layout file. Second, the bundle index leaves `NativeAsset::guid` empty for EBX entries. The new grid's import therefore resolved to null, leaving its record as an unresolved `DataDefinedTypeInstance` (`0xc3f20b2f`) instead of `GridListViewModel` (`0x7422aa5b`). The asset author now takes partition GUIDs from parsed EBX headers for the grid, imported HUD records and unchanged resident assets.
+
+Each grid tile has its own challenge action. The three stock tiles preserve their separate native navigation IDs. 1-Up owns its `open` callback; each of the eight new modes has a separate callback descriptor carrying that mode's stable ID. There is no grid-wide challenge callback. The read-only probes `../probe_throwdown_grid_resources.py` and `../probe_throwdown_picker.py` verify resolved asset types, twelve distinct logo textures, fixed native presenters, name strips, stock navigation IDs and nine distinct custom callback pointers.
+
+The delayed rebuild and inactive custom callbacks had a separate confirmed cause: the stock page briefly empties its content array during transitions. Reading element zero unconditionally threw, and cleanup cleared the callback generation before throwing again, leaving a visible grid with all nine custom callbacks disabled. The outer error path also delayed retries five seconds. `mounted_body` now checks the live page and array; transient empty content preserves the retained grid, delegates and pending actions instead of invoking cleanup. `../throwdown-retained-callbacks-before-fix.log` records the zero generations; the subsequent live probe confirmed all nine descriptors belong to the mounted page. Selection also logs the challenge callback ID. Full live gameplay and multiplayer behavior remain user validation tasks.
+
+The shared-container visibility build was installed on 2026-10-10 with a hash-verified backup at `E:/Games/ReSkate/ReSkate-OneUp-backups/native-throwdowns-abb50cec4d484195ace89b79056ed196`. Installed DLL SHA-256 is `637f41c614cc514c6b60b0fb8f9477f3214b5386d5dfd914c97453b33ffddafc`; launcher SHA-256 is `8972ca40c9da4d30a3b35bdf8457150c5b1c5931c4d81a61c4fea49af900ceaf`. The game was restarted normally without an automatic challenge command. A successful launch and passing unit checks do not establish visual correctness or multiplayer compatibility.
+
+The restarted game remained responsive and the read-only loaded-resource check passed: two native resource lists contain the private tile, native grid model and all nine private badges. Results are saved in `../throwdown-tile-visibility-live-resources.log`. The user controls opening and visually checking the picker; this asset residency check does not establish that the final rendered logos remain visible.
+
+## Full-size logo frame correction, 2026-10-10
+
+The user's next screenshot confirmed that the delayed-height fix kept all twelve logos visible, but they appeared as tiny dots inside the cells. A fresh read-only probe of the mounted picker confirmed every TextureViewModel already requested 320-by-320 dimensions. The donor `Texture_Widget` has a separate fixed 64-by-64 `RimeWidgetEntityData` root, with content-fitting disabled. The view model sizes its internal container, leaving that outer frame small in the dynamic reference.
+
+`LogoTexture_Widget` now provides a private 320-by-320 native frame for the twelve picker images. Only the donor blueprint's private name and root Width/Height change; its nineteen entity components, forty-two property connections, focus link, lifetime events, texture renderer, context bindings and resizing behavior are retained. The stock Texture widget used by other menus and gameplay HUD remains untouched. The tile's existing padded 320-by-320 logo region, lower caption strip and independent callbacks remain intact. The author reads the serialized asset back and verifies its frame dimensions and retained graph counts. The loaded-resource and picker probes now require this private logo blueprint, preventing an unnoticed fallback to the small stock frame.
